-- Insurance claim settlement (v0).
--
-- An insurer authorises claim settlements against a policy. Every
-- settlement consumes from the policy's aggregate limit; once the
-- aggregate is exhausted, no further settlements admit. The audit log
-- records who decided each payment, when, against which policy, and
-- under what authority.
--
-- The load-bearing rule is the cumulative-cap shape:
--
--   sum(already_paid_on_policy) + proposed_settlement <= aggregate_limit
--
-- This is the IR shape Expr::Add was added for. Without Add the rule
-- would have to be written as `proposed <= aggregate - already_paid`,
-- which works but contorts the natural business statement.
--
-- The proposing actor is *transition context*, not a transformation
-- parameter. authorise_settlement declares no `actor` parameter; the
-- proposing actor is consulted via the reserved term `actor` inside
-- the require for authority and stamped onto the SettlementAuthorised
-- record.

program insurance_claim_settlement

predicate Policy(policy_id: Subject, aggregate_limit: Decimal)
predicate ClaimReported(claim_id: Subject, policy_id: Subject, claimed_amount: Decimal)
predicate SettlementAuthority(actor: Subject, limit: Decimal)
predicate SettlementAuthorised(claim_id: Subject, settlement_id: Subject, amount: Decimal, actor: Subject)
predicate SettlementPaid(policy_id: Subject, claim_id: Subject, settlement_id: Subject, amount: Decimal)
predicate PolicyHeadroom(policy_id: Subject, remaining: Decimal)
predicate PolicyLimitUsage(policy_id: Subject, used: Decimal)


-- ============================================================
-- Invariant: payment must have authorisation.
--
-- An eternal consistency rule. The transformation never asserts a
-- SettlementPaid without an authorisation, but the runtime contract
-- holds against candidate state regardless of how it arrived. An
-- orphan payment is rejected by this invariant; a real corruption
-- scenario surfaces immediately rather than persisting silently.
-- ============================================================
invariant paid_implies_authorised:
    SettlementPaid(_, claim, settlement, amount) implies SettlementAuthorised(claim, settlement, amount, _)


-- ============================================================
-- Invariant: at most one PolicyHeadroom per policy.
--
-- PolicyHeadroom is the operational remaining-capacity counter -
-- distinct from Policy (the immutable contractual cap) and from
-- PolicyLimitUsage (the read-side reporting view). Two competing
-- headroom claims for the same policy would mean two answers to
-- "how much capacity remains?" - the kind of ambiguity a governed
-- model exists to forbid.
-- ============================================================
invariant at_most_one_headroom_per_policy:
    PolicyHeadroom(policy, a) and PolicyHeadroom(policy, b) implies a = b


-- ============================================================
-- Transition invariant: conservation of policy headroom.
--
-- Each newly-admitted SettlementPaid for a policy must consume the
-- corresponding decrement from that policy's PolicyHeadroom. The
-- `pre(...)` wrapper opts subtree evaluation into the
-- pre-transition state; the body compares pre-state and post-state
-- headroom against the newly-admitted payment amount.
--
-- The aggregate-limit require in authorise_settlement is the
-- admission gate ("is there enough headroom?"); this invariant is
-- the conservation law ("did the payment actually consume exactly
-- its amount?"). Both kept: a buggy transformation that staged
-- SettlementPaid without retracting and re-asserting
-- PolicyHeadroom would pass the require but fail this invariant.
--
-- Genesis: issue_policy admits initial PolicyHeadroom against an
-- empty pre-state; `pre(PolicyHeadroom(...))` matches nothing, the
-- implies is vacuously true. Once the policy exists, every
-- subsequent settlement is constrained.
-- ============================================================
invariant headroom_consumed_by_payment:
    PolicyHeadroom(p, after) and pre(PolicyHeadroom(p, before))
    and SettlementPaid(p, _, s, amt) and not pre(SettlementPaid(p, _, s, amt))
    implies after = before - amt



-- ============================================================
-- Open a policy with an aggregate limit.
--
-- Admits two claims: the immutable Policy(policy_id,
-- aggregate_limit) contractual cap, and an initial
-- PolicyHeadroom(policy_id, aggregate_limit) operational
-- remaining-capacity counter. At issuance the two coincide; no
-- settlement has yet consumed any headroom.
-- ============================================================
transformation issue_policy(policy_id, aggregate_limit):
    admit Policy(policy_id, aggregate_limit)
    admit PolicyHeadroom(policy_id, aggregate_limit)
    emit PolicyIssued(policy_id)

-- ============================================================
-- Record a reported loss against a policy. The policy must exist;
-- the claimed amount is informational - cumulative-cap reads from
-- SettlementPaid, not from this claim.
-- ============================================================
transformation report_claim(claim_id, policy_id, claimed_amount):
    require Policy(policy_id, _)
    admit ClaimReported(claim_id, policy_id, claimed_amount)
    emit ClaimReported(claim_id)

-- ============================================================
-- Grant settlement authority. Ungated in v0; administrative
-- authority for granting is out of scope.
-- ============================================================
transformation grant_settlement_authority(actor, limit):
    admit SettlementAuthority(actor, limit)
    emit SettlementAuthorityGranted(actor, limit)

-- ============================================================
-- The load-bearing transformation. Authorises a settlement against
-- a reported claim, subject to:
--
--   1. The proposing actor's settlement authority covers the
--      proposed amount.
--   2. The cumulative-cap rule holds against the policy aggregate.
--
-- bind pulls policy_id and aggregate_limit into scope from their
-- uniquely-matching claims. Where require is a yes/no existence
-- gate that does not export bindings, bind is a deterministic
-- unique lookup that does. Together with let (for value-producing
-- expressions like sum) and for (for iteration), this is the
-- statement-level binding doctrine.
-- ============================================================
transformation authorise_settlement(claim_id, settlement_id, amount):
    -- Pull policy_id from the reported claim, then aggregate_limit
    -- from the policy, then current_headroom from the operational
    -- counter. Zero matches reject lawfully; multiple matches
    -- surface a kernel error (the at_most_one_* structural-
    -- uniqueness invariants exist to prevent this).
    bind ClaimReported(claim_id, policy_id, _)
    bind Policy(policy_id, aggregate_limit)
    bind PolicyHeadroom(policy_id, current_headroom)

    -- actor authority gate
    require SettlementAuthority(actor, actor_limit) and amount <= actor_limit

    -- cumulative-cap admission gate (the Expr::Add shape). Kept
    -- alongside the headroom_consumed_by_payment transition
    -- invariant: this is the "is there enough?" check; the
    -- invariant is the "did the payment actually consume?" check.
    require sum(paid | SettlementPaid(policy_id, _, _, paid)) + amount <= aggregate_limit

    -- Conservation: compute the new headroom, retract the old
    -- claim, assert the new. The transition invariant
    -- headroom_consumed_by_payment verifies this pair landed
    -- correctly post-commit.
    let new_headroom = current_headroom - amount
    retract PolicyHeadroom(policy_id, current_headroom)
    admit PolicyHeadroom(policy_id, new_headroom)

    admit SettlementAuthorised(claim_id, settlement_id, amount, actor)
    admit SettlementPaid(policy_id, claim_id, settlement_id, amount)
    emit ClaimPaymentRequested(settlement_id, amount, actor)


-- Derived claim. Enumerated on demand via
-- `morpholog inspect derived insurance_claim_settlement PolicyLimitUsage`.
-- As-of replay reconstructs usage as it stood at any prior transition.
derived PolicyLimitUsage(policy_id):
    over SettlementPaid(policy_id, _, _, _)
    value used = sum(paid | SettlementPaid(policy_id, _, _, paid))
